1. The "Collaboration" Badges (High Priority)
These are the most important for DevOps because they prove you can work within a Git-flow environment.

🏆 Pull Shark: * What it proves: You know how to open, manage, and merge Pull Requests (PRs).

How to get it: Get 2 Pull Requests merged into a public repository. It can even be your own repository!

Levels: It "levels up" (Bronze, Silver, Gold) as you merge more PRs (16, 128, 1024).

🏆 Pair Extraordinaire:

What it proves: You can collaborate with others (essential for "Dev + Ops" culture).

How to get it: Co-author a commit on a merged PR.

The Shortcut: When committing, add Co-authored-by: name <email@address.com> to the bottom of your commit message. When that PR is merged, both of you get the badge.

2. The "Reliability" Badges
🏆 Quickdraw:

What it proves: You are responsive—a key trait for SREs and Infrastructure engineers.

How to get it: Close an issue or a PR within 5 minutes of it being opened.

🏆 YOLO:

What it proves: (A bit of a joke badge) You are comfortable taking action.

How to get it: Merge a PR without a code review. Note: Use this sparingly in your own repos just to trigger the badge; in a real job, always get a review!

3. The "Community & Knowledge" Badges
🏆 Galaxy Brain:

What it proves: You can solve problems for others—great for showing seniority.

How to get it: Have your answer in a GitHub Discussion marked as the "Accepted Answer" twice.

🏆 Starstruck:

What it proves: You created something the community finds useful.

How to get it: Create a repository that receives 16 stars.


Badges you CANNOT get anymore (Legacy)
You might see some people with the Arctic Code Vault Contributor (a snowflake icon) or the Mars 2020 Contributor.

Can you get them? No. These were awarded for contributing to specific repositories at a specific time in history (2020).

Why does this matter? Don't waste time trying to "unlock" these; they are permanently locked for anyone who wasn't active back then.
